  "summary": "Painted faces, pressed fros, and punk reclaimed: Black alternative in full color.",
  "description": "Afropunk — the movement around the 2003 documentary and 2005 Brooklyn festival — is Black alternative culture's self-assertion: natural hair architecture (fros, locs, braids as sculpture), face paint and bold geometric pattern, thrifting culture, genre-mixing music (punk, soul, house, trap), and the \"no rockists\" refusal of white alternative gatekeeping. The festival became a global platform (Paris, Johannesburg, London) and a style engine: its face-paint-and-print look, natural-hair pride and DIY-to-couture range redefined what \"punk\" and \"alternative\" can wear. Its photography tradition — the festival crowd as style documentary — is one of the most-copied street-style genres of the 2010s-20s.",
  "culturalContext": "Afropunk is Black alternative culture's self-assertion: from the 2003 documentary and 2005 Brooklyn festival to a global platform (Paris, Johannesburg), its natural-hair architecture, face paint and genre-mixing redefined what punk and alternative can look like.",
